{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KwqxK8iHov4KrAyM7j76mn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// rabadon-gate — the native hot path.\n2\t//\n3\t// One binary, zero dependencies, C++17. Reads one Claude Code hook event as\n4\t// JSON on stdin, gives its verdict via exit code (0 allow, 2 refuse), appends\n5\t// the evidence to the spool, and best-effort pushes it to a live watcher over\n6\t// the unix socket. The deterministic PreToolUse path lives here entirely; the\n7\t// cold paths (PostToolUse test analysis, diagnosis, push-gate suite runs,\n8\t// session bookkeeping) are delegated to the Node gate, which keeps its full\n9\t// behavior. Contract: SPEC.md §1 — any error in the gate itself fails OPEN.\n10\t//\n11\t// Why native: this process runs on EVERY tool call of a session. A node\n12\t// process pays its runtime startup each time; this binary answers in\n13\t// microseconds-to-low-milliseconds, measured by native/bench.mjs and\n14\t// published, not claimed.\n15\t\n16\t#include <cstdio>\n17\t#include <cstdlib>\n18\t#include <algorithm>\n19\t#include <cstring>\n20\t#include <ctime>\n21\t#include <regex>\n22\t#include <set>\n23\t#include <string>\n24\t#include <vector>\n25\t#include <fstream>\n26\t#include <sstream>\n27\t#include <sys/socket.h>\n28\t#include <sys/un.h>\n29\t#include <sys/stat.h>\n30\t#include <sys/wait.h>\n31\t#include <unistd.h>\n32\t#include <fcntl.h>\n33\t#include <csignal>\n34\t#include <cerrno>\n35\t#include <dirent.h>\n36\t\n37\t#include \"usage.h\"   // the shared token/cost meter (budget cap here + rabadon-lens)\n38\t#include \"sha256.h\"  // the hash-chained spool (emitter here + rabadon-audit)\n39\t#include \"chain.h\"    // the ledger's one writer: chained line + .head sidecar\n40\t#include \"baseline.h\" // the three laws that hold with no guard.json at all\n41\t#include \"rules.h\"   // guard.json rule parsing + matching — shared with `rabadon exec`\n42\t#include \"hookev.h\"  // ONE place that turns any agent's event into rabadon's event\n43\t#include \"moves.h\"   // R1: the move record. Recorded here, read by nothing here.\n44\t#include \"signals.h\" // R2: five detectors over that record. Silent by law.\n45\t#include \"semantic.h\"// R3: tier 1, the SHAPE of an edit. Adds a signal, never changes one.\n46\t#include \"inject.h\"  // R4: the diagnosis the agent cannot see, sized and scrubbed.\n47\t#include \"policy.h\"  // R5: repair.mode — consent decided once by `rabadon init`.\n48\t#include \"prices.h\"  // R6: the offline price table — four classes, no network.\n49\t#include \"counter.h\" // R6: the one honest line at session close, and its arithmetic.\n50\t#include \"testout.h\" // did the runner execute any tests — shared with `rabadon net`\n51\t#include \"version.h\" // one version string, lockstep with package.json\n52\t#include <sys/file.h>\n53\t#include \"cli_help.h\"\n54\t#include \"gated_client.h\" // R7: hand the call to the warm daemon if one is listening\n55\t\n56\tusing std::string;\n57\t\n58\t// Supervision mode, resolved once per invocation. WATCH is not a crippled\n59\t// ENFORCE: every rule runs and every verdict is recorded, the only difference\n60\t// is that the action is allowed to proceed. See the three-state block in main().\n61\tenum { MODE_SILENT = 0, MODE_WATCH = 1, MODE_ENFORCE = 2 };\n62\tstatic int g_mode = MODE_SILENT;\n63\t// Which agent sent this event. Set once from hookev.h, read only where a\n64\t// refusal has to be SPOKEN, because that is the one thing the agents disagree\n65\t// about: the laws are identical, the way you tell the agent \"no\" is not.\n66\tstatic rbhook::Dialect g_dialect = rbhook::DIALECT_UNKNOWN;\n67\t// argv[0], so the gate can find its sibling binaries (rabadon-net) wherever the\n68\t// install lives — a symlinked /opt/homebrew/bin must not break the net.\n69\tstatic string g_self;\n70\tstatic string self_dir() { size_t s = g_self.rfind('/'); return s == string::npos ? string(\".\") : g_self.substr(0, s); }\n71\t// The exit code a refusal turns into. Claude Code reads 2 as \"blocked\"; in watch\n72\t// mode the same refusal is recorded and then waved through with 0.\n73\tstatic int refuse_code() { return g_mode == MODE_ENFORCE ? 2 : 0; }\n74\tstatic const char* mode_tag() { return g_mode == MODE_ENFORCE ? \"enforce\" : \"watch\"; }\n75\t\n76\t// ---------- tiny JSON field extraction (enough for hook payloads) ----------\n77\t// We need a handful of known string fields. Values are JSON-escaped; we\n78\t// unescape the common sequences. Anything unparseable -> empty -> fail open.\n79\t\n80\t// Both of these now live in rules.h so the gate and `rabadon exec` read a\n81\t// rule object the same way. Thin forwards keep the ~50 call sites below\n82\t// untouched while there is exactly one implementation to audit.\n83\tstatic string json_unescape(const string& s) { return rbrules::json_unescape(s); }\n84\t\n85\tstatic string get_str(const string& j, const string& key, size_t from = 0) {\n86\t  return rbrules::get_str(j, key, from);\n87\t}\n88\t\n89\tstatic long long get_num(const string& j, const string& key) {\n90\t  const string pat = \"\\\"\" + key + \"\\\"\";\n91\t  size_t k = j.find(pat);\n92\t  if (k == string::npos) return 0;\n93\t  size_t colon = j.find(':', k + pat.size());\n94\t  if (colon == string::npos) return 0;\n95\t  return atoll(j.c_str() + colon + 1);\n96\t}\n97\t\n98\tstatic string json_escape(const string& s) {\n99\t  string out;\n100\t  for (char c : s) {\n101\t    switch (c) {\n102\t      case '\"': out += \"\\\\\\\"\"; break;\n103\t      case '\\\\': out += \"\\\\\\\\\"; break;\n104\t      case '\\n': out += \"\\\\n\"; break;\n105\t      case '\\r': out += \"\\\\r\"; break;\n106\t      case '\\t': out += \"\\\\t\"; break;\n107\t      default:\n108\t        if ((unsigned char)c < 0x20) { char b[8]; snprintf(b, 8, \"\\\\u%04x\", c); out += b; }\n109\t        else out += c;\n110\t    }\n111\t  }\n112\t  return out;\n113\t}\n114\t\n115\t// utf8_clip lives in chain.h now, beside the ledger's one writer — it was\n116\t// defined here, five call sites used it, and the seven that kept a raw substr\n117\t// wrote the corrupt day this comment used to describe.\n118\tusing rbchain::utf8_clip;\n119\t\n120\tstatic string read_file(const string& p) {\n121\t  std::ifstream f(p, std::ios::binary);\n122\t  if (!f) return \"\";\n123\t  std::ostringstream ss; ss << f.rdbuf();\n124\t  return ss.str();\n125\t}\n126\t\n127\tstatic bool file_exists(const string& p) { struct stat st; return stat(p.c_str(), &st) == 0; }\n128\t\n129\t// stat() collapses two different answers into one false: \"the flag is not\n130\t// there\" and \"I am not allowed to look\". The first is a user who never ran\n131\t// `rabadon on`. The second is a supervisor that has lost sight of its own\n132\t// switch, and reading that as watch mode is how an installed, enabled gate\n133\t// goes quiet. Measured 5 August: an unset HOME, a RABADON_DIR pointing at a\n134\t// regular file, a home at mode 000, and a home on a read-only volume each\n135\t// produced exit 0 with zero bytes on stdout for `rm -rf`, which from the\n136\t// user's seat is indistinguishable from \"rabadon looked and it was fine\".\n137\t// Every storage failure in the same run fell closed, including a genuinely\n138\t// full disk. The ledger was hard and the switch was not.\n139\tenum FlagState { FLAG_PRESENT, FLAG_ABSENT, FLAG_UNKNOWN };\n140\t\n141\t// ONE FILE FOR THE MODE. `enabled` (present/absent) and `mode.last` (the\n142\t// comparison marker) said the same thing twice, in two shapes, and a machine\n143\t// could hold one without the other. Both collapse into <RABADON_DIR>/mode,\n144\t// whose first line is exactly one of: enforce | watch | silent.\n145\t// Returns \"\" when the file is absent or says something this build cannot read —\n146\t// an unreadable switch is never quietly downgraded to watch.\n147\tstatic string read_mode_file(const string& p) {\n148\t  std::ifstream f(p);\n149\t  if (!f) return \"\";\n150\t  string w;\n151\t  std::getline(f, w);\n152\t  while (!w.empty() && (w.back()=='\\n' || w.back()=='\\r' || w.back()==' ' || w.back()=='\\t')) w.pop_back();\n153\t  size_t b = w.find_first_not_of(\" \\t\");\n154\t  if (b == string::npos) return \"\";\n155\t  w = w.substr(b);\n156\t  if (w == \"enforce\" || w == \"watch\" || w == \"silent\") return w;\n157\t  return \"\";\n158\t}\n159\tstatic FlagState flag_state(const string& p) {\n160\t  struct stat st;\n161\t  errno = 0;\n162\t  if (stat(p.c_str(), &st) == 0) return FLAG_PRESENT;\n163\t  return errno == ENOENT ? FLAG_ABSENT : FLAG_UNKNOWN;\n164\t}\n165\t\n166\t// The home is an answer only if somebody named it. With neither RABADON_DIR\n167\t// nor HOME set, \"./.rabadon\" is a guess about whatever directory the agent\n168\t// happens to be standing in.\n169\tstatic bool rabadon_home_known() {\n170\t  const char* rd = getenv(\"RABADON_DIR\");\n171\t  if (rd && rd[0]) return true;\n172\t  const char* h = getenv(\"HOME\");\n173\t  return h && h[0];\n174\t}\n175\t\n176\t// Three rules answer to the operator alone: disabled[] in guard.json does not\n177\t// switch them off, and the refusal must not offer a door that is welded shut.\n178\t// They are named here rather than at the decision site because the refusal\n179\t// printer needs the same answer. See the sealing note above the promise block.\n180\tstatic bool sealed_rule(const string& id) {\n181\t  return id == \"promise-tamper\" || id == \"promise-anti-path\" || id == \"guard-weaken\";\n182\t}\n183\t\n184\t// The rabadon home: RABADON_DIR when set (test isolation, multi-tenant), else\n185\t// $HOME/.rabadon. ONE rule for the mode flags AND the spool — a split home\n186\t// means `rabadon on` and the ledger disagree about which machine they live on.\n187\t// ONE definition, in pathres.h, because baseline.h must refuse a delete aimed\n188\t// at this directory and a law that computes the path itself can disagree with\n189\t// the program that uses it.\n190\tstatic string rabadon_home() { return rbpath::rabadon_dir(); }\n191\t\n192\tstatic long long now_ms() {\n193\t  struct timespec ts; clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);\n194\t  return (long long)ts.tv_sec * 1000 + ts.tv_nsec / 1000000;\n195\t}\n196\t\n197\t// The spool file is named after the day, so every gate call needed that string\n198\t// and every gate call built it from scratch: time() + gmtime_r() + strftime().\n199\t// reports/R7/PROFIL-YARGILAMA.md measured that one line at 28.5% of the\n200\t// daemon's ENTIRE judging cost. The formatting is not what costs: the first"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0192cvfjsyyZKrJXRYLswB6P","session_id":"81910dae-7509-4342-9a27-c75d4f682cbf","uuid":"0e1152b5-ed36-40b4-869f-b3b9e63d14ef","timestamp":"2026-08-24T13:50:53.547Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Trace counter in observe mode"}
